WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.050 --> 00:00:04.087
Welcome to the Federal Modernization
Workbook's developer workbook,

2
00:00:04.087 --> 00:00:05.688
Building Provider Notices.

3
00:00:05.688 --> 00:00:09.800
This is Chapter 5: Google Cloud's
Personalized Service Health.

4
00:00:09.800 --> 00:00:12.783
It's the smallest collector,
with the largest gaps,

5
00:00:12.783 --> 00:00:15.912
and saying plainly what it
can't see is part of the job.

6
00:00:15.912 --> 00:00:21.081
We'll start from Google Cloud's own
documentation for the events API and the viewer

7
00:00:21.081 --> 00:00:25.263
role, see what it leaves to you, then
read how the kit fills the record,

8
00:00:25.263 --> 00:00:29.900
which two gaps it writes down instead
of hiding, and why one call is refused.

9
00:00:29.900 --> 00:00:33.250
Then two labs, the
evidence and the dangers.

10
00:00:33.250 --> 00:00:34.825
Two notes before we begin.

11
00:00:34.825 --> 00:00:38.187
This chapter is a draft,
awaiting the owner's review.

12
00:00:38.187 --> 00:00:42.080
And the code is illustrative,
tested against fixtures.

13
00:00:45.830 --> 00:00:46.943
Why this chapter?

14
00:00:46.943 --> 00:00:50.878
Because Google's notices are the
narrowest of the five providers,

15
00:00:50.878 --> 00:00:54.230
and the danger is an empty list
that looks like good news.

16
00:00:54.230 --> 00:00:56.005
So the goal has two halves.

17
00:00:56.005 --> 00:01:00.789
By the end you can read a project's or an
organization's Personalized Service Health

18
00:01:00.789 --> 00:01:03.979
incidents, with the viewer
role, into records the router

19
00:01:03.979 --> 00:01:07.030
and the weekly digest treat
like any other provider's.

20
00:01:07.030 --> 00:01:11.368
And you can tell the security officer
exactly what Google's notices don't cover.

21
00:01:11.368 --> 00:01:16.037
It's for developers who maintain the kit,
the project owner who owns this gate,

22
00:01:16.037 --> 00:01:19.220
and the security officer,
who records the gaps.

23
00:01:22.970 --> 00:01:25.233
Next, which cloud and which scope.

24
00:01:25.233 --> 00:01:30.764
Unlike Azure or AWS, Personalized Service
Health has no separate government endpoint

25
00:01:30.764 --> 00:01:33.683
in the kit's table, so the
cloud is simply global,

26
00:01:33.683 --> 00:01:37.832
and each record links to the event
on Google's Service Health host.

27
00:01:37.832 --> 00:01:42.029
The scope comes from the settings: a
project, which is the call Google's

28
00:01:42.029 --> 00:01:46.520
command line was verified for, or an
organization when the settings say so.

29
00:01:46.520 --> 00:01:49.295
The API is enabled once per project.

30
00:01:49.295 --> 00:01:54.407
And for government workloads, the question
isn't an endpoint at all, but a package:

31
00:01:54.407 --> 00:01:57.505
as we'll see, Personalized
Service Health isn't in

32
00:01:57.505 --> 00:02:01.500
the Assured Workloads FedRAMP
or impact-level packages.

33
00:02:05.250 --> 00:02:06.925
Here's Google's starting point.

34
00:02:06.925 --> 00:02:10.755
Google Cloud's REST reference
documents the project events list,

35
00:02:10.755 --> 00:02:13.875
with its permission, and an
organization equivalent.

36
00:02:13.875 --> 00:02:18.888
The event resource has a category whose
only value is incident, plus a state,

37
00:02:18.888 --> 00:02:20.990
a detailed category, relevance,

38
00:02:20.990 --> 00:02:25.113
its impacts by product and
location, and its updates over time.

39
00:02:25.113 --> 00:02:26.888
Planned maintenance isn't in it.

40
00:02:26.888 --> 00:02:29.663
The API is enabled once per project.

41
00:02:29.663 --> 00:02:32.649
The Personalized Service
Health Viewer role reads it.

42
00:02:32.649 --> 00:02:36.940
And the command line lists a project's
events at the global location.

43
00:02:40.690 --> 00:02:44.502
Here's the first gap, and
it's built into Google's API.

44
00:02:44.502 --> 00:02:47.665
The event's category
has one value, incident.

45
00:02:47.665 --> 00:02:50.402
Planned maintenance isn't
in the resource at all.

46
00:02:50.402 --> 00:02:54.554
So maintenance and retirements
never arrive through this API,

47
00:02:54.554 --> 00:02:56.790
and no amount of code changes that.

48
00:02:56.790 --> 00:02:59.140
What the kit does is refuse to hide it.

49
00:02:59.140 --> 00:03:04.137
Every Google notice is recorded as an
incident, and the gap is written in the guide,

50
00:03:04.137 --> 00:03:07.344
in this chapter and in the
security officer's record,

51
00:03:07.344 --> 00:03:10.402
and it shows in the weekly
digest's provider table.

52
00:03:10.402 --> 00:03:13.727
Google's maintenance reaches
the project by other channels.

53
00:03:13.727 --> 00:03:15.708
The danger is assuming it's covered,

54
00:03:15.708 --> 00:03:18.930
and a maintenance window
landing with nobody told.

55
00:03:22.680 --> 00:03:25.330
The second gap is about
government packages.

56
00:03:25.330 --> 00:03:32.288
Google Cloud's Assured Workloads
supported-products page lists Personalized Service

57
00:03:32.288 --> 00:03:38.578
Health under twenty-five regional packages,
including the U S Data Boundary, but

58
00:03:38.578 --> 00:03:43.630
not under FedRAMP Moderate, FedRAMP High,
I L 2, I L 4, I L 5, C J I S or I T A R.

59
00:03:43.630 --> 00:03:47.048
So a workload in one of those
packages has nothing to read,

60
00:03:47.048 --> 00:03:50.393
and an empty list there looks
like good news when it isn't.

61
00:03:50.393 --> 00:03:54.562
The kit's answer, which is the program's
design guidance, is to record the

62
00:03:54.562 --> 00:03:59.750
package as a gap with the security officer,
who decides the compensating channel.

63
00:04:03.500 --> 00:04:05.225
What else does Google leave to you?

64
00:04:05.225 --> 00:04:08.556
Impacts are nested product
and location objects,

65
00:04:08.556 --> 00:04:12.537
so the kit turns them into the
record's services and regions.

66
00:04:12.537 --> 00:04:15.195
An event's description is sometimes empty,

67
00:04:15.195 --> 00:04:17.700
so the kit takes the latest update's text.

68
00:04:17.700 --> 00:04:22.525
Nothing says action is required, and
the kit adds no rule of its own here:

69
00:04:22.525 --> 00:04:27.850
an open incident that names resources
routes by the agency's rule, in Chapter 6.

70
00:04:27.850 --> 00:04:31.059
The organization call is
verified in REST but its

71
00:04:31.059 --> 00:04:34.738
command-line flags weren't
read, so it's marked unverified.

72
00:04:34.738 --> 00:04:37.251
And two response shapes are both accepted.

73
00:04:37.251 --> 00:04:41.460
The verdict: use Google's project
call and viewer role as published,

74
00:04:41.460 --> 00:04:44.430
build the mapping, and
write the gaps down.

75
00:04:48.180 --> 00:04:49.118
Now the code.

76
00:04:49.118 --> 00:04:52.656
The collector makes one declared
call: the project's events,

77
00:04:52.656 --> 00:04:56.556
or the organization's when the
settings' scope says organization.

78
00:04:56.556 --> 00:04:59.286
The normalizer keeps only incident events;

79
00:04:59.286 --> 00:05:02.018
anything else would be
left out and counted.

80
00:05:02.018 --> 00:05:04.955
An event whose state is
closed closes the record.

81
00:05:04.955 --> 00:05:07.921
Products and locations
from the event's impacts

82
00:05:07.921 --> 00:05:10.442
become the record's services and regions.

83
00:05:10.442 --> 00:05:14.867
The detailed category and relevance
are kept as the provider's severity.

84
00:05:14.867 --> 00:05:18.556
And the source link is the event
on Google's Service Health host.

85
00:05:18.556 --> 00:05:22.005
Personalized Service Health
has no separate government

86
00:05:22.005 --> 00:05:25.250
endpoint in the kit's table,
so the cloud is global.

87
00:05:29.000 --> 00:05:30.775
One call gets special treatment.

88
00:05:30.775 --> 00:05:35.033
Google's REST call for organization
events is verified against Google's

89
00:05:35.033 --> 00:05:38.439
documentation, but the command-line
group for it is listed

90
00:05:38.439 --> 00:05:41.562
on the beta reference, and
its exact flags weren't read.

91
00:05:41.562 --> 00:05:45.112
So the kit marks the call
unverified, and the live invoker

92
00:05:45.112 --> 00:05:48.963
refuses to run it unless someone
passes an explicit override.

93
00:05:48.963 --> 00:05:52.799
It stays out of the scheduled run
until someone reads those flags.

94
00:05:52.799 --> 00:05:55.950
A command run on a guess
is how a read goes wrong.

95
00:05:55.950 --> 00:05:59.296
The tests pin it: six in the
collector's Google block,

96
00:05:59.296 --> 00:06:03.570
plus the live tests for the
project command and the refusal.

97
00:06:07.320 --> 00:06:08.370
Who's involved?

98
00:06:08.370 --> 00:06:10.145
Developers maintain the kit.

99
00:06:10.145 --> 00:06:15.670
The project owner owns this gate: enabling
the API and granting the viewer role.

100
00:06:15.670 --> 00:06:20.145
And the security officer keeps the
role binding and records the two gaps.

101
00:06:20.145 --> 00:06:21.670
The permissions are short.

102
00:06:21.670 --> 00:06:24.820
The project call needs the
viewer role on the project.

103
00:06:24.820 --> 00:06:28.808
The organization call needs the
same role on the organization,

104
00:06:28.808 --> 00:06:30.495
and it's the unverified one.

105
00:06:30.495 --> 00:06:34.700
Grant at the project unless the
settings' scope really is organization,

106
00:06:34.700 --> 00:06:37.694
because a viewer role
granted at the organization

107
00:06:37.694 --> 00:06:40.545
for a project read is more
read scope than needed.

108
00:06:40.545 --> 00:06:42.750
Nothing the reader holds can write.

109
00:06:46.500 --> 00:06:51.338
Lab 1 needs only PowerShell 7 with
Pester, working in the kit's folder.

110
00:06:51.338 --> 00:06:55.213
The lab settings name a fictional
project, and nothing signs in.

111
00:06:55.213 --> 00:06:57.774
Step 1 lists what Google Cloud needs.

112
00:06:57.774 --> 00:07:03.125
The organization call carries its reason
for being unverified: verified is false.

113
00:07:03.125 --> 00:07:07.769
Step 2 collects the day-one fixtures:
two notices, both incidents,

114
00:07:07.769 --> 00:07:12.063
one open, none needing action,
both valid against the schema.

115
00:07:12.063 --> 00:07:17.933
Step 3 reads them: one open and one
closed, with products and locations taken

116
00:07:17.933 --> 00:07:23.970
from their impacts, Cloud S Q L in U S East
4, and Cloud Storage in U S Central 1.

117
00:07:27.720 --> 00:07:29.845
Step 4 collects the second week.

118
00:07:29.845 --> 00:07:33.541
The open incident has closed:
both records are closed now,

119
00:07:33.541 --> 00:07:35.507
each with the end of its window.

120
00:07:35.507 --> 00:07:41.208
Step 5 groups both weeks by kind, and
there's only one: incident, four times.

121
00:07:41.208 --> 00:07:43.470
That's the first gap made visible.

122
00:07:43.470 --> 00:07:47.948
However many weeks you collect,
maintenance never arrives through this API,

123
00:07:47.948 --> 00:07:52.284
which is exactly why the chapter writes
it down with the security officer

124
00:07:52.284 --> 00:07:56.990
rather than letting an all-incident
list suggest that nothing is planned.

125
00:08:00.740 --> 00:08:04.133
Step 6 switches the scope
to organization in memory

126
00:08:04.133 --> 00:08:07.527
and asks the live invoker
for the organization call.

127
00:08:07.527 --> 00:08:10.706
It refuses before it starts
the Google command line,

128
00:08:10.706 --> 00:08:14.106
because the call isn't verified,
and the message says why:

129
00:08:14.106 --> 00:08:17.728
the group is on the beta reference,
and its flags weren't read.

130
00:08:17.728 --> 00:08:23.134
Step 7 builds the project command a live
run would use, without running it: service

131
00:08:23.134 --> 00:08:28.378
health events list, for the fictional
project, at the global location, as JSON.

132
00:08:28.378 --> 00:08:32.891
Step 8 runs this chapter's tests:
eight passed, none failed.

133
00:08:32.891 --> 00:08:35.090
Then delete the output folder.

134
00:08:38.840 --> 00:08:41.683
Lab 2 moves to a Google
Cloud test project,

135
00:08:41.683 --> 00:08:45.278
never production, and no
incidents is a valid result.

136
00:08:45.278 --> 00:08:46.647
As the project owner,

137
00:08:46.647 --> 00:08:50.677
enable the API and grant the
viewer role to the reader's group.

138
00:08:50.677 --> 00:08:52.377
Sign in as the reader.

139
00:08:52.377 --> 00:08:54.465
Cross-check with Google's own command:

140
00:08:54.465 --> 00:08:57.091
its count and the kit's
collection should agree.

141
00:08:57.091 --> 00:08:59.850
Then the step that matters
most in this chapter:

142
00:08:59.850 --> 00:09:02.327
record the gaps with the security officer.

143
00:09:02.327 --> 00:09:07.027
Planned maintenance doesn't come through
this API, and a project in an Assured

144
00:09:07.027 --> 00:09:12.166
Workloads FedRAMP or impact-level folder
has no Personalized Service Health at all.

145
00:09:12.166 --> 00:09:16.030
Finally, remove the role
binding and delete the run.

146
00:09:19.780 --> 00:09:24.580
Each output is evidence for something
specific, against NIST's control catalog.

147
00:09:24.580 --> 00:09:26.898
The viewer role, and nothing that writes,

148
00:09:26.898 --> 00:09:29.918
supports monitoring an
external service provider.

149
00:09:29.918 --> 00:09:32.760
The run folder shows
every incident was read,

150
00:09:32.760 --> 00:09:36.830
which also supports receiving
security alerts and advisories.

151
00:09:36.830 --> 00:09:39.105
A call nobody verified isn't run.

152
00:09:39.105 --> 00:09:42.666
And the gaps written down support
updating the risk assessment,

153
00:09:42.666 --> 00:09:46.967
which is the evidence this chapter adds
that no other provider chapter needs.

154
00:09:46.967 --> 00:09:48.671
The indicators are FedRAMP's,

155
00:09:48.671 --> 00:09:52.643
and which output supports which
indicator is the program's mapping.

156
00:09:52.643 --> 00:09:57.690
The kit's records support these controls;
the agency's assessor decides them.

157
00:10:01.440 --> 00:10:04.315
What breaks in production,
and what guards against it?

158
00:10:04.315 --> 00:10:06.732
Assume Google's maintenance is covered,

159
00:10:06.732 --> 00:10:10.614
and a maintenance window lands
with nobody told; the guard is the

160
00:10:10.614 --> 00:10:14.715
gap written in the record, the digest
and the security officer's file.

161
00:10:14.715 --> 00:10:19.120
Expect Personalized Service Health in
a FedRAMP or impact-level workload,

162
00:10:19.120 --> 00:10:23.383
and an empty list looks like good news;
the guard is recording the package,

163
00:10:23.383 --> 00:10:26.865
so the security officer can
choose a compensating channel.

164
00:10:26.865 --> 00:10:28.600
Run the unverified call,

165
00:10:28.600 --> 00:10:32.852
and you've run a command whose
flags nobody read; it's refused.

166
00:10:32.852 --> 00:10:36.342
And grant the viewer role at the
organization for a project read,

167
00:10:36.342 --> 00:10:38.600
and you've granted more than needed.

168
00:10:42.350 --> 00:10:43.700
Here's what done looks like.

169
00:10:43.700 --> 00:10:45.941
The API is enabled on the project,

170
00:10:45.941 --> 00:10:49.262
and the reader's group
holds only the viewer role.

171
00:10:49.262 --> 00:10:53.026
Google's list and the kit's
collection agree in the test project.

172
00:10:53.026 --> 00:10:55.694
The organization call stays unverified,

173
00:10:55.694 --> 00:10:59.463
and out of the scheduled run,
until someone reads its flags.

174
00:10:59.463 --> 00:11:01.401
The test binding was removed.

175
00:11:01.401 --> 00:11:04.849
And the two gaps are written
down: planned maintenance,

176
00:11:04.849 --> 00:11:09.525
and any workload in an Assured Workloads
FedRAMP or impact-level package.

177
00:11:09.525 --> 00:11:13.959
Next, Chapter 6 routes what all
five collectors gather to owners,

178
00:11:13.959 --> 00:11:15.800
as Ticket-to-Machine tasks.

179
00:11:15.800 --> 00:11:16.960
Thanks for watching.
